  "headline_findings": [
    "Open-loop governance changed the primary diagnosis in at most 0.0% of cases: the layers annotate but do not alter clinical decisions.",
    "No governed variant differs from baseline in diagnostic accuracy (paired Wilcoxon, all p >= 0.05).",
    "Exact McNemar on binary correctness vs V1: 0 of 5 comparisons significant after Holm-Bonferroni over 30 paired tests (smallest raw p 0.211609); accuracy differences range -0.0133 to 0.0333.",
    "Closed vs open loop (V5-CL - V5, n=300): accuracy 0.43 -> 0.43; revision fixed 42 and broke 42 answers (exact McNemar p=1.0, Holm 1.0).",
    "88-102% of each governed variant's reported quality drop comes from penalizing its own detectors: the 'Audit Paradox' is a scoring artifact.",
    "No governance signal detects misdiagnosis meaningfully better than chance: AUROCs range 0.497-0.555 (0.5 = chance).",
    "The safety validator raised at least one alert on 100% of runs, so alert presence carries no information about whether the diagnosis is wrong.",
    "Power: with 300 paired cases and 0.21 mean discordance, the smallest accuracy difference detectable at alpha 0.05 / power 0.8 is 0.0738; a 5-point difference needs 657 pairs (171 baseline misdiagnoses).",
    "JRU is not reported as a measurement: no run was scored by a judge model different from the generator.",
    "Model-reported diagnosis probability: ECE 0.2374, Brier 0.3021 (n=297).",
    "Latency bottleneck: Diagnosis Agent (mean 16.8s, p95 32.8s)."
  ]
